var BANK_PARTS = BANK_PARTS || [];
BANK_PARTS.push([
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"In a coastal lab, mice given a kelp supplement gained more weight over eight weeks than mice in the same room that were not given it. The director concludes that the kelp supplement caused the extra weight gain.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats the supplement and the extra gain occurring together as sufficient to show that the supplement caused the gain","takes for granted that no mouse can gain weight unless it receives the kelp supplement","rejects the director's conclusion on the ground that the lab sells kelp products","draws a conclusion about every coastal lab from results in a single room","infers that the group of mice has a trait shown for only one mouse"],"a":0,"e":"The only support offered is that the supplemented mice and the extra gain went together, and that pairing is treated as causation. The attractive choice — takes for granted that no mouse can gain weight unless it receives the kelp supplement — loses because the argument never says the supplement is required for any weight gain. It says this supplement caused the extra gain in this room. A necessary-condition claim about all mice is a stronger claim than the one being made."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Equivocation","d":2,"s":"A critic called the quartet's recording \"live,\" meaning the players performed together in one room. The prize rules reserve the live-recording prize for a recording made at a public concert. The producer concludes that the recording is eligible because the critic called it live.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["appeals to the critic as an authority on which recordings deserve the prize","infers eligibility merely because the review and the rules appeared in the same month","generalizes from this recording to every recording the quartet has made","uses the critic's sense of \"live\" as if it were the prize rules' sense of \"live\"","rejects the prize rules because the organizers might gain from a sold-out concert"],"a":3,"e":"One sense of \"live\" is a shared room and the other is a public concert. The eligibility claim treats them as the same sense. The attractive choice — appeals to the critic as an authority on which recordings deserve the prize — loses because the producer does not treat the critic as a prize judge. The producer treats the critic's word as if it satisfied a rule that uses the same word for a public concert."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The new soil test is accurate because it agrees with the farmer's notebook, and the notebook is accurate because it agrees with the new soil test.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the notebook caused the test results merely because the two agree","lets the test and the notebook certify each other","draws a conclusion about every soil test from this one test","treats a smaller share of matching rows as a smaller number of matching rows","appeals to the farmer's standing in the county"],"a":1,"e":"Each source is the only support offered for the other, so neither accuracy claim is independently established. The attractive choice — infers that the notebook caused the test results merely because the two agree — loses because agreement is being used as mutual proof of accuracy, not as a claim that the notebook produced the results. A causal reading adds a conclusion the argument does not draw."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The outdoor stage has held the summer opera every year for fifteen years. The producer concludes that it will hold this year's opera.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the stage caused the opera's artistic success merely because the two occurred together","treats one opera company as representative of every arts group","appeals to a famous singer's endorsement of the stage","lets this year's contract and last year's contract certify each other","projects that run of success forward without showing that this year's load matches the earlier ones"],"a":4,"e":"Fifteen successful years support this year only if this production is relevantly like the earlier ones, and that likeness is not given. The attractive choice — infers that the stage caused the opera's artistic success merely because the two occurred together — loses because the argument does not claim the stage caused artistic success. It claims a structure that held past productions will hold the next one."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":2,"s":"Adding limestone to the east meadow reduced soil acidity there. Therefore, adding limestone to the bog will reduce soil acidity in the bog.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the limestone caused the meadow result merely because the two happened in the same season","treats each plot in the meadow as having a feature that only the whole meadow has","transfers a result from the meadow to the bog without establishing a relevant likeness","appeals to a celebrity gardener's endorsement of limestone","treats the absence of a contrary bog study as proof the bog will respond"],"a":2,"e":"A meadow fix is handed to a bog. Nothing shows the soils match in the way that made the limestone work. The attractive choice — infers that the limestone caused the meadow result merely because the two happened in the same season — loses because the meadow result is a premise, not a correlation the argument is trying to prove. The unsupported step is carrying that result over to the bog."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":2,"s":"Dr. Shah argues that the clinic should replace its entry rugs, and cites slip counts. A board member notes that Shah's sibling sells mats, and concludes from that note alone that the rugs need not be replaced.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["sets the slip counts aside because of a relative's business, rather than because of a problem in the counts","treats the sibling as a medical authority on entryway slips","infers that mats reduce slips merely because both were mentioned in the same month","generalizes from this clinic's entry to every clinic in the region","treats keeping the rugs and replacing them as if a third option had been ruled out by the sibling's job"],"a":0,"e":"The only reason given for dropping the slip counts is who would gain. That is a comment on Shah's household, not on the counts. The attractive choice — treats the sibling as a medical authority on entryway slips — loses because the sibling is not cited as an expert. The sibling's sales are cited as a reason to ignore Shah."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each instrument in the quintet is quiet enough for the chapel. Therefore, the quintet playing together is quiet enough for the chapel.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats being quiet enough as necessary for playing in the chapel and then as sufficient for being invited","rejects the choirmaster because the choirmaster rents instruments","treats the lack of a noise complaint as proof the quintet is quiet","gives the ensemble a feature established only for each instrument","projects last Sunday's volume onto next Sunday without a new fact"],"a":3,"e":"Five quiet instruments can be loud together. The chapel limit is applied to the ensemble just because it fits each player. The attractive choice — treats being quiet enough as necessary for playing in the chapel and then as sufficient for being invited — loses because the argument does not mention invitations or a rule for being asked to play. It moves from each instrument's volume to the group's volume."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"At the mill, oak was 40 percent of the boards shipped last year and 22 percent this year. The buyer concludes that fewer oak boards were shipped this year.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats last year's oak share as proof that the same share will return","ignores the possibility that total boards shipped rose enough to offset oak's smaller share","dismisses the buyer because the buyer owns an oak lot","gives the whole shipment a feature shown only for one board","uses \"oak\" for the tree in the premise and the color in the conclusion"],"a":1,"e":"Twenty-two percent of a much larger shipment can be more oak boards than forty percent of a small one. The attractive choice — treats last year's oak share as proof that the same share will return — loses because the conclusion is not a prediction that the share will rebound. It is a claim that the number of oak boards fell."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"Every January rental on the lake is winterized. Cabin 9 is winterized. Therefore, cabin 9 is rented in January.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that winterizing caused the January rental market, because winterizing and January are both discussed","generalizes from cabin 9 to every cabin on the lake, though the conclusion names only cabin 9","rejects the rental office because the agent prefers summer leases","treats winterizing and leaving the cabin empty as the only two winter plans","treats a necessary condition for a January rental as if it were sufficient"],"a":4,"e":"January rental requires winterizing. The argument notices winterizing and concludes there is a January rental. The attractive choice — infers that winterizing caused the January rental market, because winterizing and January are both discussed — loses because the argument is about this cabin, not about what caused the rental market. The error is running \"January only if winterized\" backward."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Post hoc","d":2,"s":"The gallery hung a red curtain on Friday. On Saturday ticket sales fell. The manager concludes that the curtain caused the drop in sales.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["takes for granted that ticket sales cannot fall unless a curtain is changed","rejects the manager because the manager owns a fabric shop","treats the curtain as the cause of the sales drop solely because the curtain was hung first","generalizes from this gallery to every gallery in the city","reads a smaller share of Saturday sales as a smaller number without a total, though no share is given"],"a":2,"e":"The curtain went up, then sales fell. That order is the entire case for causation. The attractive choice — takes for granted that ticket sales cannot fall unless a curtain is changed — loses because the manager does not say a curtain change is required for any sales drop. The manager says this curtain caused this drop, and the only reason is the order of the two events."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Courtyards that ran their fountains had more songbird visits the next spring than courtyards that left fountains off. The director concludes that the fountains caused the extra visits.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Nothing other than the fountains accounts for the difference in songbird visits.","Any courtyard that runs a fountain will gain songbirds, whatever else is true of the courtyard.","Songbirds prefer courtyards that also contain benches.","Most courtyards in the region have no fountain.","Fountains use less water than lawn sprinklers."],"a":0,"e":"If some other difference produced the extra visits, the causal conclusion fails. The argument therefore depends on no other difference accounting for the gap. The attractive choice — Any courtyard that runs a fountain will gain songbirds, whatever else is true of the courtyard. — loses because that universal future rule is stronger than the argument needs. The conclusion is about the visits that already differed. It can stand if some other courtyard would not gain birds, so long as nothing else explains this gap."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Seed packets fell from half the nursery's items sold to 30 percent. The buyer concludes that fewer seed packets were sold.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The nursery sold no items other than seed packets and flowering plants.","Seed packets cost less than flowering plants.","Most customers who buy seed packets buy nothing else.","Total items sold did not rise enough to offset seed packets' smaller share.","The same clerk recorded both periods."],"a":3,"e":"A lower share is a lower number only when the total does not grow enough to cancel it. Negate that and the buyer's count can fail while every premise stays true. The attractive choice — The nursery sold no items other than seed packets and flowering plants. — loses because limiting the shop to two products does not pin down the count. A two-product shop can sell more packets if the total jumps. The conclusion needs the total not to have swamped the smaller share, not a particular product list."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":3,"s":"Dr. Feldman, author of the regional manual on harbor pilings, says the elm pilings should be replaced this year and offers no other reason. The board concludes that the elm pilings should be replaced this year.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whatever Feldman says on any subject is a sufficient reason for the board to act.","Feldman's manual and training bear on whether these elm pilings should be replaced.","Feldman is also a popular speaker at boating festivals.","The manual was first published twenty years ago.","Several board members have not read the manual."],"a":1,"e":"If Feldman's expertise does not bear on these pilings, the bare citation does not support replacement. The argument depends on that bearing. The attractive choice — Whatever Feldman says on any subject is a sufficient reason for the board to act. — loses because a blanket endorsement of Feldman is more than the argument needs. The conclusion is about these pilings. It fails if Feldman's expertise does not bear on them, even if Feldman is unreliable about unrelated subjects. The needed assumption is the relevant bearing, not omniscience."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":3,"s":"The orchard manager says: either the trees are netted, or the birds will ruin the crop. The trees will not be netted. Therefore, the birds will ruin the crop.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Netting is the most effective bird control that exists for any crop.","Netting is the most expensive protection on the price list.","The manager dislikes the look of netting.","Some fruit buyers prefer netted orchards.","There is no way, other than netting, to keep the birds from ruining this crop."],"a":4,"e":"\"No netting, therefore ruin\" fails if another protection would save this crop. The argument depends on there being no such protection. The attractive choice — Netting is the most effective bird control that exists for any crop. — loses because the manager does not need netting to be best in the world. The manager needs there to be no other way for this crop. A cheaper tape that works here would sink the conclusion even if netting is best somewhere else, and a world-beating net is not required if nothing else works here."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":2,"s":"The clerk says the clock-tower bell is on time because the bell is on time. From that remark alone, a listener concludes that the clerk's remark is adequate evidence for the claim.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The bell is in fact on time.","The bell was cast in another town.","A claim offered as its own only ground can be adequate evidence for that claim.","Listeners enjoy the sound of the bell.","The tower is the oldest building on the square."],"a":2,"e":"If a self-grounded claim cannot be adequate evidence for itself, the listener's conclusion fails while the clerk's remark stays as it is. The argument depends on that loop counting as evidence. The attractive choice — The bell is in fact on time. — loses because the listener is judging the remark as evidence, not reporting that the bell happens to be right. The bell can be on time while this remark is still empty, and the remark's adequacy does not require us to assume the bell is right. Truth of the bell is a different question from whether this loop counts as evidence."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"On one midday crossing, most passengers who were asked said a dawn ferry should be added. The scheduler concludes that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["That midday crossing matches the ferry's passengers overall in the reasons they ride.","Midday passengers are mostly tourists who would not board at dawn.","The ferry runs fewer crossings in winter than in summer.","A few passengers on that crossing said no.","Dawn trips cost more to staff than midday trips."],"a":0,"e":"If the crossing does not match the ridership in why people ride, \"most of those asked\" does not reach \"most passengers.\" The conclusion depends on the match. The attractive choice — Midday passengers are mostly tourists who would not board at dawn. — loses because a tourist-only crossing would undermine the generalization. The scheduler does not need that undermining fact. The scheduler needs the crossing to stand in for the ridership. The attractive choice is a weaken, not a required assumption."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No inspection log for the linen closet reports moths. The housekeeper concludes that the closet contains no moths.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Other closets in the house have been free of moths.","Moths are more common in wool than in cotton.","The closet is on the second floor.","If the closet contained moths, an inspection would have reported them.","The housekeeper wrote some of the logs."],"a":3,"e":"Silence shows an empty closet only if moths would have appeared in the log. Without that, the housekeeper's conclusion does not follow. The attractive choice — Other closets in the house have been free of moths. — loses because other closets are a tempting clean record, but this conclusion is about this closet. Their condition is not required. A moth-free house next door would not establish a search of these shelves, and a moth in another closet would not by itself create one here."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"Every approved mural grant was reviewed by the arts board. This grant was reviewed by the arts board. Therefore, this grant was approved.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Some grants reviewed by the arts board were approved.","Every grant reviewed by the arts board was approved.","The arts board meets on Fridays.","This grant is popular with the neighbors.","Approved grants are printed on cotton paper."],"a":1,"e":"The premise gives the board's review and a one-way link from approval to review. Adding the converse, that review yields approval, forces this grant to have been approved. The attractive choice — Some grants reviewed by the arts board were approved. — loses because \"some\" leaves this grant undecided. The review could be one of the grants that were not approved. The full converse, that every reviewed grant was approved, is what ties this review to approval."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"A phone ban reduced noise in the library reading room. Therefore, a phone ban will reduce noise in the hospital wards.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["The reading room is quieter than a hospital café.","Some wards already ask visitors to whisper.","Some wards already ask visitors to speak softly, which says nothing about whether a reading-room ban will transfer.","The library bought new chairs the same month.","Any phone ban that reduces noise in a reading room will reduce noise in these hospital wards."],"a":4,"e":"The ban's reading-room result plus a rule that such results transfer to these wards yields the conclusion. Nothing weaker in the other choices builds that bridge. The attractive choice — The reading room is quieter than a hospital café. — loses because a comparison with a café does not move the reading-room result into the wards. The sufficient link is a rule that carries this kind of ban from the reading room to these wards."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each pump is under the quiet limit. Therefore, the fountain as a whole is under the quiet limit.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["People who live by the plaza describe the fountain as relaxing.","Residents near the plaza say they enjoy the sound of moving water.","The fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump.","The pumps are painted green.","One of the pumps is new."],"a":2,"e":"The premises put every pump under the limit. Equating the fountain with its loudest pump carries that limit to the whole. The attractive choice — People who live by the plaza describe the fountain as relaxing. — loses because a neighbor's description does not cap the decibels. If the fountain is only as loud as its loudest pump, quiet pumps entail a quiet fountain."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The path was underwater this morning. Therefore, the irrigation line was left open overnight.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["The path is underwater in the morning only if the irrigation line was left open overnight.","An open irrigation line can leave a path underwater.","The irrigation line is painted green.","The groundskeeper has worked in the garden for years.","Paths in other gardens were dry this morning."],"a":0,"e":"A wet morning plus \"wet only if the line was left open\" entails that the line was left open. Possibility alone does not. The attractive choice — An open irrigation line can leave a path underwater. — loses because \"can\" shows the favored cause is possible, which does not rule out rain. \"Only if\" leaves the open line as necessary for the flood, so a wet morning yields the open line."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Most maintainers of the east path want more gravel. Therefore, most users of the east path want more gravel.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Maintainers own the shovels used on the path.","Gravel is cheaper than wood.","The path runs along the east creek.","If most maintainers of the east path want more gravel, then most users of that path want more gravel.","Some users were not asked about gravel."],"a":3,"e":"The maintainers' majority is given. The conditional exports it to users. That is sufficient for the conclusion. The attractive choice — Maintainers own the shovels used on the path. — loses because owning shovels does not transfer a majority from maintainers to users. The conditional from one majority to the other does."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Most passengers on one midday ferry crossing said they wanted a dawn trip. The scheduler concludes that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Only passengers who already commute at dawn were handed the question card.","Passengers on that crossing match the ferry's passengers overall in why they ride and how often they ride.","The ferry runs fewer crossings in winter than in summer.","A few people on that crossing said the schedule should stay as it is.","Dawn trips cost more to staff than midday trips."],"a":1,"e":"A crossing that matches the ridership in purpose and frequency can speak for passengers in general. That is the likeness the generalization needs. The attractive choice — Only passengers who already commute at dawn were handed the question card. — loses because handing cards to dawn riders makes the sample a worse stand-in for the whole ridership. It talks about who was asked, which is the live issue, and then biases the sample the wrong way."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"No shift log mentions a jammed dome motor. The director concludes that the motor has not jammed.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The director wrote some of the shift logs.","The dome motor is louder than the projector fan.","A jammed motor is expensive to replace.","Visitors rarely ask about the dome.","Every jam of that motor would have been entered in the shift log, and the logs for the period are complete."],"a":4,"e":"If a jam would be logged and the log is complete, silence is evidence of no jam rather than a gap in the news. The attractive choice — The director wrote some of the shift logs. — loses because authorship uses the logs and the person drawing the conclusion. It does not show that a jam would have been written down or that every page is present. A complete, sensitive log does."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each pump is under the quiet limit. The engineer concludes that the fountain as a whole is under that limit.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The fountain is louder than its loudest pump because the basin adds its own noise.","The plaza also has a produce market.","The fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump, and no other part of the fountain makes sound.","The quiet limit was set by the town council rather than by the engineer.","Fountains with several pumps are often described as relaxing."],"a":2,"e":"If the fountain is only as loud as its loudest pump, pumps under the limit put the fountain under the limit. The attractive choice — The fountain is louder than its loudest pump because the basin adds its own noise. — loses because a noisy basin uses the parts-and-whole relation and reverses it. The whole would then be louder than the pumps already measured, which leaves the conclusion worse off, not better."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The cellar floor was wet this morning. The keeper concludes that the sump pump was left off overnight.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["No pipe burst, the floor dries within an hour of any spill, and a pump left off is the only other way this cellar floods.","A pump left off can leave a cellar floor wet.","The pump is painted the same green as the cellar door.","The keeper has worked in the building for many years.","Cellars in the next street were dry this morning."],"a":0,"e":"With rain and spills set aside, and with a stopped pump as the only remaining listed cause, the wet floor points to the pump. The attractive choice — A pump left off can leave a cellar floor wet. — loses because \"can\" restates the keeper's favored cause as a possibility. The argument already treats that cause as the conclusion. Ruling the rivals out is what strengthens it. Possibility does not."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":2,"s":"The tide table is called reliable because it matches the harbormaster, who is called reliable because of the tide table. A clerk concludes that the table's reliability has been established.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The table and the harbormaster agree with each other.","The harbormaster is well known among boaters.","The table is printed in large type.","An independent gauge, calibrated last week and not set from either source, matches both the table and the harbormaster.","Boaters say they like the harbormaster."],"a":3,"e":"A gauge set from neither source is support that does not lean on the circle. Agreement inside the circle is what was already inadequate. The attractive choice — The table and the harbormaster agree with each other. — loses because their agreement is the circle already on the page. Repeating it feels like confirmation and adds no outside check. The gauge is outside the pair."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"Shade cloth stopped leaf scorch on the herb beds. The gardener concludes that the same cloth will stop leaf scorch on the greenhouse benches.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The greenhouse is larger than the herb beds.","The scorch in both places comes from the same midday sun on the same kind of leaves, and the cloth blocks that sun in both places.","The herb beds were covered in June.","The greenhouse already has a fan.","Shade cloth is sold in several colors."],"a":1,"e":"Same sun, same leaves, same block: the herb-bed result is then a reason to expect the same result on the benches. The attractive choice — The greenhouse is larger than the herb beds. — loses because a size difference sounds like a reason to hesitate, which is the live worry about an analogy, but size alone does not say whether the sun and the leaves match. The shared sun and leaves do."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The kiln reached the target temperature on every firing this month. The potter concludes that it will do so on the next firing.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The potter bought a new apron before the next firing.","The kiln sits in a brick studio.","Some firings last year ran cool.","Customers prefer evenly fired pots.","The next firing uses the same clay, the same load, and the same fuel setting as the firings that reached the target."],"a":4,"e":"The successful firings and the next one share the settings that govern temperature. An apron does not. The attractive choice — The potter bought a new apron before the next firing. — loses because a new apron is a change in the shop and feels like a reason to re-check the prediction. It is not a feature of the firing. Matching clay, load, and fuel is what puts the next firing inside the record."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Anyone who finishes the dock course gets a green badge. Riley has a green badge. Therefore, Riley finished the course.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Riley was seen with dock-course materials the week the badge was issued.","The course is offered only on weekdays.","Green badges are also issued to inspectors who never take the course, and Riley is an inspector.","Most graduates wear the badge on a lanyard.","The course was shortened last year."],"a":2,"e":"Finishing is enough for a badge, not the only way. An inspector's badge that never required the course leaves Riley's badge unexplained by the course. The attractive choice — Riley was seen with dock-course materials the week the badge was issued. — loses because materials make Riley look like a student, so they seem to speak to whether Riley finished. They lean toward the conclusion. A second route to the badge, with Riley on that route, is what blocks the inference."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":2,"s":"The lane beside the greenhouse was underwater this morning. The gardener concludes that the drip line was left open overnight.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["A storm dropped three inches of rain overnight, and the drip valve stayed closed.","An open drip line can leave a lane underwater.","The drip line is painted green to match the beds.","The gardener has worked in the greenhouse for many years.","Lanes in two other gardens were dry this morning."],"a":0,"e":"Rain supplies a cause, and a closed valve removes the gardener's cause. The underwater lane no longer points to an open drip line. The attractive choice — An open drip line can leave a lane underwater. — loses because that is the gardener's own cause, offered as a possibility. Showing the favored cause can happen does not weaken a conclusion that already picks it. Rain with a closed valve points elsewhere and rules the line out."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"A weekday matinee audience mostly called the play relaxing. The reviewer concludes that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The matinee was sold out.","The reviewer prefers plays described as relaxing.","Tickets cost the same in the evening as at the matinee.","Weekday matinees are mostly retirees, while the theater's other shows draw a younger crowd that has called the play tense.","A few matinee viewers did not answer the question."],"a":3,"e":"If the other audiences are different and call the play tense, one matinee cannot stand for theatergoers. A sold-out room does not fix who was in the room. The attractive choice — The matinee was sold out. — loses because a full house feels like a solid sample. It says nothing about whether matinee viewers differ from theatergoers at other shows. The age split and the other crowd's reaction do."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":3,"s":"A marathon champion says the city should fluoridate its drinking water. Therefore, the city should fluoridate its drinking water.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Some residents dislike the taste of fluoridated water.","Regional water engineers studied this supply and recommended that it not be fluoridated.","The champion has won marathons in several countries.","A neighboring city fluoridates its drinking water.","The champion drinks this city's tap water every day."],"a":1,"e":"Relevant specialists recommending against fluoridation undercut a conclusion that rested only on a runner's say-so. The attractive choice — Some residents dislike the taste of fluoridated water. — loses because taste is a real reaction to the policy and uses the policy's subject. It is a preference, not a finding about whether this supply should be fluoridated. The engineers' recommendation is that finding, and it cuts against the conclusion."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"A critic called the mural \"original,\" meaning a new design. The town protects an \"original,\" meaning the first physical panel. A councillor concludes the mural is protected because the critic called it original.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The councillor personally admires the mural.","The critic's column appears on Fridays.","The heritage rule was written in the last century.","Murals can be larger than the wall that holds them.","The critic was not talking about the first physical panel, and this mural is a later copy of an older design."],"a":4,"e":"If the critic's word does not mean the first panel, and this mural is not that panel, the shared word does not trigger the rule. The attractive choice — The councillor personally admires the mural. — loses because admiration feels like a reason someone would call the mural protected. It does not show that the critic's \"original\" meets the rule, and it does not separate the two senses. A later copy that the critic was not calling the first panel does separate them."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Towns that opened weekend markets saw honey sales rise in those same weeks. A clerk concludes that the markets caused the honey-sales rise.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The markets sell honey.","The clerk personally likes street markets.","Those same weeks were the only weeks the clover bloomed, and honey sales track the bloom even in towns with no market.","Some towns without markets sold a small amount of honey.","The markets are held outdoors."],"a":2,"e":"If sales follow the bloom where there is no market, the shared weeks are a poor reason to credit the markets. The attractive choice — The markets sell honey. — loses because honey on the stalls fits the clerk's story and seems to explain the rise. A bloom that tracks sales in towns with no market is a rival cause the stalls do not answer."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Most people who stayed on the museum's donation line supported a new wing. The fundraiser concludes that most visitors support a new wing.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Callers who stay on the donation line are already the museum's largest donors, and other visitors mostly oppose the wing.","The museum is free on Sundays.","The phone line plays recorded music while callers wait.","The proposed wing would add two galleries.","Some callers said they were in a hurry."],"a":0,"e":"If the people who stayed on the line are the big donors and everyone else opposes the wing, the line cannot stand for visitors. The attractive choice — The museum is free on Sundays. — loses because Sunday admission feels like a fact about who visits. It does not show that the callers differ from visitors on the wing. A donor-only phone line, plus opposition among other visitors, does."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"A clerk may open the sealed folios only if that clerk wears linen gloves. Dr. Berg wears linen gloves today. The sealed folios include the 1912 mill map.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Dr. Berg may open the sealed folios today.","Anyone wearing linen gloves may open the sealed folios.","The 1912 mill map may be opened without linen gloves.","If Dr. Berg opens the 1912 mill map today, then Dr. Berg is wearing linen gloves.","Dr. Berg will open the 1912 mill map today."],"a":3,"e":"Opening a folio requires the gloves. So if Dr. Berg opens the mill map, the gloves are on. Nothing in the statements gives Dr. Berg permission, a plan, or a glove-free exception. The attractive choice — Dr. Berg may open the sealed folios today. — loses because gloves are required, not enough. Wearing them is what a permitted clerk must do. It does not confer permission. The attractive choice runs the rule backward."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"Principle: a criticism that cites only a plan's author's motive does not, by itself, show that the plan is unwise. Maren's only criticism of the reservoir plan is that its author wants to be reelected.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The reservoir plan is unwise.","Maren's criticism does not, by itself, show that the reservoir plan is unwise.","The author of the plan offered no engineering evidence.","Maren wants the reservoir plan to fail.","Any plan whose author wants to be reelected is unwise."],"a":1,"e":"Maren's remark is a motive-only criticism, and the principle says exactly that sort of remark does not show the plan is unwise. The attractive choice — The reservoir plan is unwise. — loses because the principle refuses to treat this kind of criticism as a verdict. The attractive choice is that verdict. Motive-only criticism leaves the plan's wisdom unsettled."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"That a ferry ran without delays last summer does not, by itself, show that the same schedule will run without delays this summer. The memo's only reason for predicting no delays this summer is last summer's record.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The schedule will have delays this summer.","The schedule had delays last summer.","Last summer's weather will be repeated this summer.","The memo compares this ferry with a different kind of boat.","The memo's reason does not, by itself, show that the schedule will run without delays this summer."],"a":4,"e":"Last summer is the memo's only reason, and that kind of reason does not by itself show a delay-free summer. Nothing in the statements predicts delays either. The attractive choice — The schedule will have delays this summer. — loses because a failure of proof is not a prediction of delay. The attractive choice turns \"not established\" into \"will be late.\" The principle does not say the ferries will be late."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"A feature of each part need not be a feature of the whole. Each of the four cases is light enough for one person to carry. Nothing further is said about their combined weight.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The full set is too heavy for one person to carry.","The full set is light enough for one person to carry.","It does not follow that the full set is light enough for one person to carry.","One of the four cases is heavy.","The cases will be carried separately."],"a":2,"e":"Light cases do not, by the principle, establish a light set, and no combined weight is given. So the set's being light enough does not follow. Its being too heavy does not follow either. The attractive choice — The full set is too heavy for one person to carry. — loses because the principle blocks the leap to the set. It does not prove the set is over the limit. \"Too heavy\" is the vivid picture of four cases. The statements only withhold the inference."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional chain","d":2,"s":"If a grant is awarded, it is listed in the red ledger. If it is listed in the red ledger, the sponsor has filed a conflict form. Grant 12 has no conflict form on file. Grant 4 is listed in the red ledger.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Grant 12 was not awarded.","Grant 4 was awarded.","Grant 12 has a conflict form in a file other than the one described.","No grant listed in the red ledger was awarded.","Grant 4's sponsor failed to file a conflict form."],"a":0,"e":"Awarded implies listed, and listed implies a form, so awarded implies a form. Grant 12 has no form, so grant 12 was not awarded. Grant 4's listing does not run the first conditional backward. The attractive choice — Grant 4 was awarded. — loses because being listed is necessary, given the chain, for an award. It is not sufficient. Grant 4's listing does not show an award. Grant 12's missing form does show there was no award, because an award would have produced a form."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"The only people asked about a dawn ferry were passengers on one midday crossing, and most of them said yes. The scheduler has not compared that crossing with the rest of the ridership.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","Most passengers oppose a dawn trip.","Midday passengers never commute.","The midday answers do not yet settle whether most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip.","A dawn trip would lose money."],"a":3,"e":"One uncompared crossing cannot yet stand for the ridership, so the yes answers do not settle the passengers' majority. They also do not show the opposite majority. The attractive choice — Most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip. — loses because a majority on the crossing is easy to export to the whole ridership, and that export is exactly what has not been earned. Without a comparison, the answers leave the wider majority unsettled. They do not establish it."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"The test used on the blue canoe would have revealed any hull crack, the whole hull was tested, and no crack was found.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The canoe cannot crack later.","The test is positive evidence that the hull had no crack at the time of the test.","The outfitter has no cracks in any other canoe.","The test was not worth running.","A crack was found and then left out of the report."],"a":1,"e":"A complete test that would have shown any crack, and did not, is evidence of no crack then. Later cracks and other canoes are outside that result. The attractive choice — The canoe cannot crack later. — loses because a clean, sensitive test supports no crack then. It says nothing about a later collision or a later crack. \"Cannot crack later\" keeps the good news and drops the time limit."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":3,"s":"The fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump, no other part makes sound, and every pump is under the town's quiet limit.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The produce market beside the plaza is under the quiet limit.","Each pump is over the quiet limit.","The fountain is the loudest machine in town.","The pumps make no sound at all.","The fountain as a whole is under the quiet limit."],"a":4,"e":"If the fountain is only as loud as its loudest pump and every pump is under the limit, the fountain is under the limit. The market is a different object. The attractive choice — The produce market beside the plaza is under the quiet limit. — loses because the market sits next to the facts and is easy to paint with the same limit. The noise rule is about the fountain. The market is not a pump and is not the fountain."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"Green badges of this kind are issued only to people who finish the dock course. Riley holds one of those badges.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["Everyone who finished the dock course holds a green badge.","Riley is an inspector who skipped the course.","Riley finished the dock course.","Riley will take the course next year.","The course is offered only on weekdays."],"a":2,"e":"If badges of this kind go only to finishers, Riley's badge means Riley finished. Finishers without badges are still possible. The attractive choice — Everyone who finished the dock course holds a green badge. — loses because \"only if\" runs from the badge back to the course, so Riley's badge yields Riley's finish. It does not run forward from every finisher to a badge. The attractive choice is the converse, which the rule does not give."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":2,"s":"An independent gauge, not set from the tide table or the harbormaster, matches both. The table and the harbormaster also match each other.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["There is support for the table's reliability that does not rest only on the harbormaster.","The table and the harbormaster prove each other without any outside check.","The gauge was set from the table.","Neither source agrees with the gauge.","Reliability cannot be checked by any instrument."],"a":0,"e":"A gauge set from neither source agrees with the table. That is support beyond the harbormaster. The mutual match is extra, not the whole case. The attractive choice — The table and the harbormaster prove each other without any outside check. — loses because the gauge is an outside check. The attractive choice describes the circle as if the gauge were not there. The facts include the gauge, so the support is not only mutual."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Weekday matinees draw a narrower crowd than the theater's other shows. From a matinee poll alone, a reviewer concludes that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing. That poll is the reviewer's only evidence, and a narrower crowd does not establish the wider claim.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Weekday matinees draw a narrower crowd than the theater's other shows.","The play is relaxing for nearly all theatergoers.","Theatergoers never enjoy matinees.","The reviewer has not established that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","The reviewer dislikes polls."],"a":3,"e":"The argument uses the narrow crowd to reject the reviewer's leap. The conclusion is that the leap is not established, not that the play is relaxing and not that matinees are unpleasant. The attractive choice — Weekday matinees draw a narrower crowd than the theater's other shows. — loses because the narrow crowd is the premise that does the work. The point drawn from it is that the reviewer's wider claim is not established. Repeating the premise misses the conclusion."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":3,"s":"Last summer's delay-free season is the memo's only reason to predict a delay-free season this summer. A past season, by itself, does not establish that prediction.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Last summer was delay-free.","The memo does not establish that this summer will be delay-free.","This summer will have delays.","Ferries are a kind of boat.","The memo was written in the spring."],"a":1,"e":"The argument says the only reason on offer does not establish the prediction. It does not predict delays. The attractive choice — Last summer was delay-free. — loses because last summer is the premise. The conclusion is that the memo's prediction is not established. Turning the premise into the main point, or turning the gap into \"there will be delays,\" both miss the sentence the argument is driving at."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":3,"s":"Phone noise was the reading room's problem, and a ban reduced it. Ward noise is mostly monitors, and phones there are already silent. So the reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Ward noise is mostly monitors and carts.","A phone ban reduced reading-room noise.","Hospitals should ban monitors.","Reading rooms and wards are identical.","The reading-room result is a poor reason to expect quieter wards."],"a":4,"e":"The \"so\" sentence limits what the reading-room ban shows about wards. The monitor fact is why that limit is drawn. The attractive choice — Ward noise is mostly monitors and carts. — loses because the monitors are a premise. They support the claim that the reading-room result will not travel. The argument does not conclude that hospitals should ban monitors."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Maintainers match users on the gravel question, and most maintainers want more gravel. So there is reason to think most users want more gravel.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Most maintainers want more gravel.","Maintainers match users on the gravel question.","There is reason to think most users want more gravel.","Every user wants more gravel.","Gravel is cheaper than wood."],"a":2,"e":"The \"so\" gives a reason about most users. The match and the maintainers' votes are the support. \"Every user\" is not the claim. The attractive choice — Most maintainers want more gravel. — loses because the maintainers' majority is a premise. The conclusion exports a reason to believe something about users. \"Every user\" would be stronger than the \"most\" the argument actually draws."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"The reviewer polled only a weekday matinee and concludes that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["drawing a conclusion about a large group from a narrower group that has not been shown to match it","treating a condition necessary for enjoying the play as if it were sufficient","showing that two reviews cite only each other","projecting a decade of full houses one year forward","comparing a reading room with a hospital ward"],"a":0,"e":"One matinee's opinion is assigned to theatergoers generally. That is a sample treated as the wider group. The attractive choice — treating a condition necessary for enjoying the play as if it were sufficient — loses because no enjoyment rule is stated. The attractive choice is a familiar conditional flaw wearing the play's topic. The actual move is from one audience to theatergoers."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Either the pond is aerated or the fish will die. The pond will not be aerated. Therefore, the fish will die.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that each fish has a feature the whole pond may lack","citing silence in a log as proof that the fish are healthy","matching a sample of ponds to every pond in the county","treating two options as exhaustive and inferring the second from the rejection of the first","applying a specialist's manual to a measurement"],"a":3,"e":"Aeration and death are treated as the only options, and refusing aeration is used to infer death. The attractive choice — showing that each fish has a feature the whole pond may lack — loses because no feature is moved from fish to pond. The attractive choice is a parts-and-whole label on a pond sentence. The method is an either-or treated as complete."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"A pastry chef says the cables should be replaced. Therefore, they should be replaced.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that a test would have found any crack in the cables","treating an endorsement from outside the relevant field as sufficient for the decision","fixing the number of cables so that a share drop becomes a count drop","offering a third way to hold the bridge together","matching a sample of bridges to a national stock of bridges"],"a":1,"e":"Replacement is inferred from a chef's say-so. That is an appeal to an endorsement outside the field. The attractive choice — showing that a test would have found any crack in the cables — loses because no test is described. The attractive choice is a good absence-of-evidence argument that this paragraph does not make. The whole reason is the chef."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"No patrol note mentions a crack, so the seawall has no crack. Whether a crack would have been noted is not said.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["equating a midday sample of sailors with every user of the harbor","hiring a novelist to judge the wall","adding streetlights and counting collisions","carrying one case and then concluding about four","treating the absence of reports as proof of absence"],"a":4,"e":"Missing notes are the entire ground for \"no crack.\" Silence is being treated as a finding. The attractive choice — equating a midday sample of sailors with every user of the harbor — loses because nobody was sampled. The attractive choice is a sampling label on a silence argument. The ground is the missing notes."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"Weekday matinees draw a narrow crowd. **Nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.** That is what the reviewer concludes from one matinee.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the only premise.","It is an objection to the reviewer.","It is the conclusion the reviewer draws.","It is a definition of \"matinee.\"","It is evidence that the matinee was empty."],"a":2,"e":"The sentence is identified as the reviewer's conclusion. The narrow crowd, not this sentence, is the material offered as support. The attractive choice — It is the only premise. — loses because the relaxing-play sentence is what the reviewer is trying to establish. The poll is the support. Swapping premise and conclusion is the attractive miss."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"**The trees will not be netted.** The manager says that either they are netted or the crop will be ruined, and concludes that the crop will be ruined.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the premise that rejects one of the two options.","It is the conclusion that the crop will be ruined.","It is a third option the manager offers.","It is a definition of netting.","It is evidence that the crop is already saved."],"a":0,"e":"The manager rejects netting and infers ruin. The bold sentence is that rejection, not the inference. The attractive choice — It is the conclusion that the crop will be ruined. — loses because \"will not be netted\" is easy to hear as the grim ending. The grim ending is ruin. Not netting is the rejected option, used as a premise."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"A pastry chef says the cables should be replaced. **The cables should be replaced.** The board draws that conclusion from the chef's remark alone.","q":"The statement in bold plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise reporting the chef's professional duties.","It is an objection by an engineer.","It is a definition of \"cable.\"","It is the conclusion.","It is evidence that the chef declined to comment."],"a":3,"e":"The board concludes the bold sentence. The chef's remark is the premise. The attractive choice — It is a premise reporting the chef's professional duties. — loses because the replacement sentence is what a reader has just seen the chef say, so it is easy to leave it in the chef's mouth as background. The board adopts it as the conclusion. The chef's job is not what the sentence states."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: if a park tree is diseased and it threatens visitors, the city must remove it. The oak by the gate is diseased and threatens visitors. The elm by the pond is diseased and threatens no one. The maple is healthy.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The city must remove the elm by the pond.","The city must remove the oak by the gate.","The city must remove the maple.","The city must remove every tree in the park.","The city must not remove the oak."],"a":1,"e":"The oak meets both conditions, so the principle requires its removal. The elm misses the threat, and the maple misses both. The attractive choice — The city must remove the elm by the pond. — loses because the elm is diseased, which is half the principle, and disease is the word a quick reading chases. The principle also requires a threat to visitors. The elm has none. The oak has both."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: a past season of on-time ferries does not, by itself, establish that the next season will be on time. The memo predicts an on-time season solely because last season was on time.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The next season will be late.","The memo establishes the prediction.","Ferries should be retired.","Last season was late.","The memo does not establish that the next season will be on time."],"a":4,"e":"Last season is the memo's only reason, and the principle says that reason does not establish the prediction. The attractive choice — The next season will be late. — loses because the principle says this sole reason fails. It does not predict lateness. The attractive choice converts a gap in proof into a forecast of delay."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: if the number of tickets stays the same and complaints' share of those tickets falls, then the number of complaints falls. Complaints' share fell, and the number of tickets stayed the same.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The number of complaints rose.","The number of tickets fell.","The number of complaints fell.","The share of complaints rose.","The desk received no tickets."],"a":2,"e":"Both conditions of the principle are met, so the number of complaints fell. The share did not rise, and the ticket total did not fall. The attractive choice — The number of complaints rose. — loses because a smaller share sounds, to a hasty arithmetic, as if it might hide a larger count. The principle says that when the total is fixed, the smaller share is a smaller count. \"Rose\" fights the principle."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: if a critic's only criticism is the author's motive, that criticism does not show the plan is unwise. Maren's only criticism of the reservoir plan is that its author wants to be reelected.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["Maren's criticism does not show that the reservoir plan is unwise.","The reservoir plan is wise.","The reservoir plan is unwise.","Maren's criticism shows the plan is unwise.","No plan proposed by someone with a motive can be wise."],"a":0,"e":"Maren's criticism is motive-only, so the principle says it does not show the plan is unwise. Wisdom itself is not settled. The attractive choice — The reservoir plan is wise. — loses because the principle blocks one criticism from proving the plan unwise. It does not declare the plan wise. \"Wise\" is the cheerful opposite of the verdict the criticism failed to earn."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: if a test would have found any crack and the whole hull was tested, a negative result establishes that there was no crack then. That is what happened with the blue canoe yesterday.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The canoe can never crack later.","Every canoe in the boathouse was sound yesterday.","The test found a crack.","The test establishes that the blue canoe's hull had no crack yesterday.","Negative results never establish anything."],"a":3,"e":"The test meets the principle's two conditions and was negative, so it establishes no crack then. Later years and other canoes are not included. The attractive choice — The canoe can never crack later. — loses because the principle is limited to the hull at the time of the test. \"Never later\" keeps the clean result and erases the time limit."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"The market and honey sales rose together, and nothing else is cited. So the clerk has not established that the market caused the rise.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["If two things rise together, one of them caused the other.","Joint occurrence, by itself, does not establish causation.","Markets cause every nearby sales rise.","Clerks are authorities on causation.","Honey sales never change without a market."],"a":1,"e":"The argument treats the overlap as inadequate for a cause. That is the principle it uses. The attractive choice — If two things rise together, one of them caused the other. — loses because that is the rule the clerk hoped for and the argument denies. Similar subject, opposite direction. The argument's principle is that overlap is not enough."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"Netting will not be used, but reflective tape, which will be used, has saved similar crops. So it does not follow that the crop will be ruined.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["There are only two possible futures for any crop.","Skipping the most expensive option always ruins a crop.","Orchard managers often dislike the look of reflective tape.","Orchard managers are always right.","Rejecting one protective option does not establish failure when another protective option is in place."],"a":4,"e":"Tape is a third option already planned, so refusing nets does not establish ruin. That is the principle the argument applies. The attractive choice — There are only two possible futures for any crop. — loses because the argument's work is to deny that two futures exhaust the case. The attractive principle asserts the exhaustion the argument rejects."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The only support for replacing the cables is a pastry chef's remark, and the decision is about cables. So the replacement has not been established.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Any famous person's remark establishes an engineering decision.","Pastry chefs are cable experts.","An endorsement from outside the field of the decision does not, by itself, establish the decision.","Cables should be replaced whenever anyone comments on them.","Fame is the same thing as a relevant engineering standard."],"a":2,"e":"The argument treats an out-of-field endorsement as insufficient. That is the principle it conforms to. The attractive choice — Any famous person's remark establishes an engineering decision. — loses because fame-is-enough is the rule the argument refuses. It mentions fame and engineering and reaches the opposite result from the argument."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The log would have recorded any jam, it is complete, and it records none. So there was no jam.","q":"The argument most closely conforms to which one of the following principles?","c":["Silence establishes absence when any instance would have been recorded and the record is complete.","Silence always establishes absence.","Silence never establishes absence.","The motor was installed by the firm that still services it.","Motors cannot jam."],"a":0,"e":"The argument reaches \"no jam\" from silence plus the two conditions. The principle includes those conditions. A blanket \"always\" does not fit the argument that needed them. The attractive choice — Silence always establishes absence. — loses because the argument uses silence only with a complete sensitive log. \"Always\" drops the two conditions the argument actually relies on. The conclusion would not have been drawn from silence alone."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The library's reading room got noisier in the month after phones were banned there.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Phones were banned in the reading room that month.","The ban was announced a week before it took effect, and compliance was high.","Reading rooms are expected to be quieter than lobbies.","The ban coincided with a ventilation fault louder than the phone noise it replaced, and the fault started that month.","The library added more chairs, which do not themselves produce the kind of noise that increased."],"a":3,"e":"A fault louder than the old phone noise, starting with the ban, raises the room's noise even though the phones are gone. The attractive choice — Phones were banned in the reading room that month. — loses because the ban is the puzzling event, not the resolution. A new, louder source explains a rise in noise after the phones went quiet."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The archive that started opening previously unchecked drawers reported more silverfish the next quarter.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The inspections were made more thorough.","The newly opened drawers held silverfish that the old inspections, which never opened them, could not have reported.","The new inspections used the same definition of a silverfish finding as the old ones.","The archive's temperature and humidity were unchanged from the previous quarter.","Staff were asked to report silverfish, and they say they complied."],"a":1,"e":"Finds rise if the new search reaches a place the old search skipped and that place has silverfish. Thoroughness then discovers rather than creates the insects. The attractive choice — The inspections were made more thorough. — loses because more thorough inspections are the puzzling cause. The resolution is where the new thoroughness looked: drawers that had never been opened and that held the insects."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The kiln that was serviced to hold a steadier temperature varied more from firing to firing after the service.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The kiln was serviced before the variation increased.","The service included the steps the kiln manual lists for steadier firing.","The potter used the same clay before and after the service.","The studio's room temperature was stable across the firings.","The service replaced a worn sensor with a miscalibrated one, and the potter followed the new sensor."],"a":4,"e":"Following a miscalibrated sensor would make the readings, and the adjustments based on them, less steady. That fits a post-service decline. The attractive choice — The kiln was serviced before the variation increased. — loses because the service is the event that needs explaining, not the explanation. A bad replacement sensor explains worse variation after a service meant to help."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The museum that dropped its entry fee had fewer visitors the next month.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Free entry is usually attractive to visitors.","The museum advertised the free month in the city paper.","The fee drop happened during the month the only road to the museum was closed for bridge work.","A visitor survey that month found the exhibits as well liked as in the paid month.","The museum did not shorten its opening hours."],"a":2,"e":"If people cannot reach the door, a lower fee does not increase visits. The closure lets the fee drop and the visit drop stand together. The attractive choice — Free entry is usually attractive to visitors. — loses because the usual pull of a free door is why the drop is surprising. A closed road explains fewer arrivals despite a more attractive price. The usual pull does not."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The historical society that digitized its diaries lent fewer diaries to researchers that year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Researchers used the digital copies, and the lending desk counts only physical loans.","The diaries were digitized that year.","The digital copies were harder to search than the physical diaries.","The society's membership, which supplies most researchers, grew that year.","The lending rules for physical diaries became more generous, not stricter."],"a":0,"e":"If use moved to copies and the desk counts only physical loans, the loan tally falls even though researchers are reading the diaries. The attractive choice — The diaries were digitized that year. — loses because digitizing is the setup. A shift to copies the desk does not count explains fewer recorded loans while access may have risen. The digitizing itself is not the resolution."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":2,"s":"Green badges of this kind are issued only to people who finish the dock course. Riley holds one. Therefore, Riley finished the dock course.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["Anyone who passes the glaze exam receives the kiln certificate. Sam passed the glaze exam. Therefore, Sam receives the certificate.","Most badge holders finish a course. Riley holds a badge. Therefore, Riley probably teaches the course.","Riley's badge is green. Therefore, every badge is green.","The kiln certificate is issued only to potters who pass the glaze exam. Sam holds the kiln certificate. Therefore, Sam passed the glaze exam.","Sam passed the exam and holds no certificate. Therefore, the exam is not required."],"a":3,"e":"Both move from \"issued only to those who meet a condition\" plus possession to the conclusion that the condition was met. The attractive choice — Anyone who passes the glaze exam receives the kiln certificate. Sam passed the glaze exam. Therefore, Sam receives the certificate. — loses because the trap runs a sufficient condition forward. The stimulus runs an \"only\" rule backward, from the badge to the course. Sam's certificate does that. Sam's passing the exam does the opposite direction."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"The test would have found any crack, the whole hull was tested, and no crack was found. Therefore, there was no crack then.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["No jam is logged. Therefore, there was no jam.","Any jam would have been logged, the log is complete, and no jam is logged. Therefore, there was no jam.","There was no jam. Therefore, the log is complete.","The log mentions a jam. Therefore, the canoe test would have found a crack.","Motors are loud. Therefore, a visitor would notice a jam."],"a":1,"e":"Both say a complete sensitive check found nothing, and both conclude the thing was absent. The attractive choice — No jam is logged. Therefore, there was no jam. — loses because silence alone is the flawed shortcut. The stimulus adds a complete test that would have caught the problem. The log choice that includes those two conditions matches. Silence by itself does not."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":3,"s":"This float is no heavier than floats the bridge has carried, and the bridge carried those floats. Therefore, there is reason to expect the bridge to carry this float.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["The kiln reached the target all month. Therefore, it must reach the target next time, whatever the load.","The next firing will fail. Therefore, past firings failed.","Cedar carries floats. Therefore, every bridge is cedar.","A heavier float was carried once. Therefore, every heavier float will be carried.","The next firing uses the same load and fuel as firings that reached the target, and those firings reached it. Therefore, there is reason to expect the next firing to reach the target."],"a":4,"e":"Both rely on a relevant match to past successes and conclude only that there is reason to expect success, not that success is guaranteed. The attractive choice — The kiln reached the target all month. Therefore, it must reach the target next time, whatever the load. — loses because the trap drops the matched conditions and upgrades \"reason to expect\" into \"must.\" The stimulus keeps the match and claims only a reason to expect. The firing choice that keeps both matches."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Parallel reasoning","d":2,"s":"Last summer is the memo's only reason, and a past summer does not by itself establish the next one. Therefore, the memo does not establish a delay-free summer.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its reasoning to the argument above?","c":["The bridge carried ten floats. Therefore, it will carry a heavier one.","The memo is about ferries. Therefore, the bridge will fail.","A decade of lighter floats is the marshal's only reason, and that record does not by itself establish success with a heavier float. Therefore, the marshal does not establish that the bridge will carry this heavier float.","Heavier floats are unsafe. Therefore, last summer had delays.","Any past success establishes any future success."],"a":2,"e":"Both take a past record as the only reason, note that the record does not by itself establish the future claim, and conclude that the future claim is not established. The attractive choice — The bridge carried ten floats. Therefore, it will carry a heavier one. — loses because the trap draws the future success the stimulus refuses to draw. The marshal choice, like the memo, stops at \"does not establish.\""},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":2,"s":"Honey sales and the weekend market rose in the same weeks. Therefore, the market caused the rise.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["Lantern sales and the evening concerts rose in the same weeks. Therefore, the concerts caused the lantern rise.","Lantern sales rose after the harbor was closed, and the closure was the only change that week. Therefore, the closure is a candidate cause.","The concerts were canceled. Therefore, lantern sales will fall.","Each lantern is light. Therefore, a crate of lanterns is light.","A lighting expert measured the lanterns. Therefore, the measurement should be ignored because the expert sells lamps."],"a":0,"e":"Both infer a cause from nothing but co-occurrence. The harbor closure is not that pattern, because it adds an only-change premise. The attractive choice — Lantern sales rose after the harbor was closed, and the closure was the only change that week. Therefore, the closure is a candidate cause. — loses because the trap adds \"only change,\" which supplies a control the flawed stimulus never has. Both the stimulus and the concert choice use timing alone. A candidate cause with a control is a different, more careful move."},
{"t":"Parallel flaw","pattern":"Parallel flaw","d":2,"s":"A famous chess champion says the town dam should be lowered. Therefore, the town dam should be lowered.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["The author of the dam manual applies the manual's rule to this dam's measurements. Therefore, there is relevant support for following that rule.","The dam is concrete. Therefore, a chess player should not comment.","Either the dam is lowered or the town floods. It will not be lowered. Therefore, the town floods.","A marathon champion says the tap water should be fluoridated. Therefore, it should be fluoridated.","The champion's remark matches an engineer's measurements. Therefore, the remark is not the only support."],"a":3,"e":"Both treat a celebrated non-specialist's say-so as enough for a technical decision. The manual application does not. The attractive choice — The author of the dam manual applies the manual's rule to this dam's measurements. Therefore, there is relevant support for following that rule. — loses because the trap supplies a relevant standard. The stimulus and the fluoridation choice rely on an outsider alone. A manual applied to measurements is not that flaw."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":3,"s":"The bridge carried ten years of floats. The marshal concludes that it will carry this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the float uses the same fabric flowers as last year","Whether this year's float is as easy on the bridge as the floats already carried","Whether the marshal owns a dress hat","Whether the parade is popular","Whether cedar is a kind of wood"],"a":1,"e":"The projection depends on this float being like the earlier loads. Decorations do not answer that dependence. The attractive choice — Whether the float uses the same fabric flowers as last year — loses because flowers can match or differ without changing the load. Either answer leaves the prediction equally supported. A heavier float would weaken it, and a comparable float would leave it standing."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":3,"s":"A clipboard cut lost badges at the craft museum. The director concludes that it will do so at the science hall.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether the science hall has a gift shop","Whether clipboards are made of wood","Whether the director's title includes the word \"chief\"","Whether badges can be blue","Whether badges are lost for the same reason, at a similarly staffed desk, in both places"],"a":4,"e":"The analogy is open until the losses are compared. A gift shop does not compare them. The attractive choice — Whether the science hall has a gift shop — loses because a gift shop can exist or not without saying whether the losses have the same cause. A shared cause would support the transfer, and a different cause would weaken it."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"Either the gallery hires a night guard or the drawings will be stolen. It will not hire a night guard. Therefore, the drawings will be stolen.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether night guards are expensive","Whether the curator dislikes night shifts","Whether the drawings can be kept safe by some measure other than a night guard","Whether drawings are made on paper","Whether the gallery building is old"],"a":2,"e":"The either-or stands or falls on whether a third measure exists. Expense does not answer existence. The attractive choice — Whether night guards are expensive — loses because price can be high or low and the drawings can still be safe or unsafe without a guard. Another working protection would sink the inference, and the absence of any other protection would support it."},
{"t":"Evaluate","pattern":"Evaluating an argument","d":2,"s":"A phone ban reduced reading-room noise. The director concludes that a phone ban will reduce ward noise.","q":"Which one of the following would be most useful to know in order to evaluate the argument?","c":["Whether ward noise has the same source the ban reduced in the reading room","Whether the hospital building is larger than the library","Whether the library bought new chairs that month","Whether some visitors dislike bans","Whether noise can be annoying"],"a":0,"e":"The transfer is useful to test at the cause of the noise. A larger building is a difference that may not matter, and the question does not say whether it matters. The attractive choice — Whether the hospital building is larger than the library — loses because size can differ and the sources can still match, or match in size and differ in source. The source decides whether the ban touches the ward's noise. Square footage does not."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":2,"s":"Chen: The matinee poll shows that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing. Duran: The matinee poll shows what that matinee thought, not what nearly all theatergoers think.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["a matinee poll was taken","the play was performed","theatergoers exist","the matinee poll shows that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing","Chen and Duran mentioned a poll"],"a":3,"e":"Chen says the poll shows the wide claim. Duran says it does not. That is the disagreement. The attractive choice — a matinee poll was taken — loses because both are talking about the same poll, so its existence is common ground. They disagree on whether it reaches nearly all theatergoers."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Ida: The clipboard that cut losses at the craft museum will cut losses at the science hall. Jules: The craft museum losses were from an unattended desk, and the science hall losses are from loading-dock theft, so the clipboard result does not carry over.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the craft museum used a clipboard","the craft museum clipboard result shows that the science hall will lose fewer badges","the science hall is a building","badges can be lost","Ida and Jules named two sites"],"a":1,"e":"Ida affirms the transfer. Jules denies it. The clipboard's having been used is background. The attractive choice — the craft museum used a clipboard — loses because both are discussing that clipboard result, so its existence is not the fight. Ida says it transfers. Jules says the different cause blocks the transfer."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":3,"s":"Ruth: There are no silverfish, because no inspection reported any. Sam: The inspections never opened the map drawers, so the lack of reports does not show that there are no silverfish.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["inspections occurred","the archive has map drawers","silverfish can be discussed","Ruth and Sam mentioned reports","the lack of reports shows that there are no silverfish"],"a":4,"e":"Ruth treats the silence as proof of absence. Sam denies that it is proof. That is the disagreement. The attractive choice — inspections occurred — loses because both are responding to the same inspections. Their existence is not in dispute. Ruth says the silence shows absence. Sam says it does not, because the drawers were never opened."},
{"t":"Point at issue","pattern":"Point at issue","d":2,"s":"Yara: The tent is a way to hold the festival, so it is false that the only options are canceling it or moving it into the hall. Zev: Canceling it and moving it into the hall are the only options, and the tent is not a way to hold the festival.","q":"The statements provide the most support for the claim that the two speakers disagree over whether","c":["the festival might be moved into the hall","a hall was mentioned","the tent is a way to hold the festival","canceling a festival is something a person might propose","Yara and Zev spoke about options"],"a":2,"e":"Yara says the tent can hold the festival. Zev says it cannot. The hall's being mentionable is not the clash. The attractive choice — the festival might be moved into the hall — loses because Zev lists the hall as an option, and Yara's target is the claim that the hall and cancellation are the only options. Yara need not deny that the hall is a way. Zev denies that the tent is a way. That denial is what Yara rejects."}
]);
